
Tree Data Structure -

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

                    A                    ← Root (no parent)
                   / \
                  /   \
                 B     C                 ← Children of A; Internal nodes
                / \     \
               /   \     \
              D     E     F              ← D, E are children of B; F is child of C
             / \           \
            /   \           \
           G     H           I           ← G, H are children of D; I is child of F

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                     
Terminology Illustrated:

Root:           A (topmost node, no parent)

Internal Nodes: A, B, C, D, F (have at least 1 child)

Leaf Nodes:     E, G, H, I (no children, degree = 0)

Parent-Child:
  - A is parent of B, C
  - B is parent of D, E
  - D is parent of G, H
  - C is parent of F
  - F is parent of I

Children:
  - B, C are children of A
  - D, E are children of B
  - G, H are children of D
  - F is child of C
  - I is child of F

Siblings:
  - B and C (same parent A)
  - D and E (same parent B)
  - G and H (same parent D)

Subtree at B:   B, D, E, G, H (B + all its descendants)

Ancestors of G: A, B, D (path from root to G)

Descendants of A: B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I (all nodes below A)

Degree:
  - Degree of A = 2 (children: B, C)
  - Degree of B = 2 (children: D, E)
  - Degree of D = 2 (children: G, H)
  - Degree of C = 1 (child: F)
  - Degree of F = 1 (child: I)
  - Degree of leaves (E, G, H, I) = 0

Levels:
  Level 0: A
  Level 1: B, C
  Level 2: D, E, F
  Level 3: G, H, I

Height:
  - Height of tree = 3 (longest path from root to leaf)
  - Height of leaf = 0

